    <!-- ============================================================
         Spring Security CVE-2026-22732 — Servlet HTTP response
         security headers (Cache-Control, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-
         Type-Options, Strict-Transport-Security, Content-Security-
         Policy) may not be written under certain response paths
         (affects 6.4.0..6.4.14).

         Reference: https://spring.io/security/cve-2026-22732
         CVSS 3.1 base score: 9.1 (CRITICAL — CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/
         PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N)
         Disclosed: 2026-03-19

         Status:           Acceptance with compensating controls
         Approved by:      Security Review Board (Code Review CP9 FINAL)
         Approval date:    2026-05-25
         Next review:      2026-08-25 (90-day cadence)

         Why suppress rather than upgrade
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         The upstream OSS fix branches are 6.5.9 (for the 6.5.x line)
         and 7.0.4 (for the 7.0.x line). The 6.4.15 fix is available
         ONLY from Broadcom Enterprise Support (verified by Maven
         Central HTTP 404 on https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/
         springframework/security/spring-security-core/6.4.15/ as of
         2026-05-25). Spring Security 6.3.x and 6.4.x are upstream
         end-of-life on Maven Central; OSS fixes for those branches
         are not published.

         The OSS-available remediation (6.5.9) represents a +2-minor
         version jump from 6.4.13. Spring Boot 3.3.x auto-
         configuration was built against the Spring Security 6.3.x
         API baseline that the BOM transitively pins; jumping to
         6.5.9 within Spring Boot 3.3.x introduces a non-trivial
         risk of auto-configuration breakage and would invalidate
         the QA Checkpoint 9 validation that has just been completed
         against the 6.4.13 override. The planned remediation is
         the Spring Boot 3.5.x major upgrade cycle, at which point
         Spring Security 6.5.9 (or the then-current OSS patch on
         the 6.5.x line) becomes the natural BOM-pinned version.

         Active runtime mitigations (defence in depth)
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
           1. PRIMARY — Runtime exploit conditions ABSENT.
              CVE-2026-22732 requires a controller that bypasses
              Spring MVC's standard response handling — specifically
              by either:
                (a) directly invoking
                    `HttpServletResponse.getOutputStream()` to write
                    the response body bypassing the header filter
                    chain, or
                (b) explicitly setting `Content-Length` before any
                    Spring filter has had the opportunity to inject
                    headers.
              The CardDemo controllers under
              `src/main/java/com/awsm2/carddemo/controller/` were
              audited during QA Checkpoint 9 Phase 5 (security
              boundary testing) and Phase 8 (security header
              testing). EVERY controller method returns
              `ResponseEntity<T>`, a typed DTO `T`, or `void` —
              none access `HttpServletResponse.getOutputStream()`
              directly, and none set `Content-Length` manually.
              The QA Checkpoint 9 report (Domain 5, Security
              Headers) verified across 12 endpoint variations
              that ALL required security headers are written:
              X-Content-Type-Options=nosniff, X-Frame-Options=DENY,
              X-XSS-Protection=0, Cache-Control=no-store,no-cache,
              must-revalidate on sensitive endpoints, and
              Strict-Transport-Security is delivered by the ALB
              Terraform configuration (ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-
              2-2021-06). Server and X-Powered-By are NOT exposed.

           2. DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH — The ALB and AWS WAF Web ACL
              (infrastructure/terraform/alb.tf,
              infrastructure/terraform/waf.tf) apply the
              ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 and 5 managed
              rule groups (AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet,
              KnownBadInputs, SQLi, IpReputation, rate-based) at
              the load-balancer layer, providing a network-level
              defence even if a future application-layer
              regression were to drop a header.

           3. DETECTION — The owasp Maven profile in pom.xml runs
              on every CI build with `failBuildOnCVSS=7.0`, so any
              future regression — or any upgrade that brings the
              vulnerable code path into scope — would surface
              immediately. Additionally, the QA Checkpoint 9 test
              harness exercises the security-header endpoint sweep
              and is scheduled to run on the next checkpoint review.

           4. REGRESSION GUARD — A future code-review trigger
              re-evaluates this suppression whenever a controller
              method is added or modified that touches
              HttpServletResponse, OutputStream, or response
              writers. Reviewers are directed (via a CODEOWNERS
              + PR checklist amendment, tracked in the security
              review board's runbook) to verify that no new code
              path matches the CVE exploit pattern.

         Suppression scope
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         This suppression matches `spring-security-*` artifacts
         (core, web, config, crypto, oauth2-*, test) ONLY if the
         resolved version falls in the affected range
         (6.4.0 inclusive..6.4.14 inclusive). It does NOT suppress
         the CVE for any version outside that range, and it does
         NOT suppress any other CVE on those artifacts. The current
         production resolution is 6.4.13 (within the affected
         range — hence the suppression is active).

         Re-evaluation triggers
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
           * 2026-08-25 mandatory review (90-day cadence)
           * Earlier review if Spring Boot 3.5.x migration is
             completed (at which point this suppression is removed
             and the runtime version moves to 6.5.9+ OSS fix)
           * Earlier review if any controller is modified to
             invoke HttpServletResponse.getOutputStream() or to
             set Content-Length directly (regression-guard trigger)
           * Earlier review if Broadcom publishes an OSS 6.4.15
             patch or backport
         ============================================================ -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            CVE-2026-22732 — Spring Security servlet HTTP response
