    <!-- ============================================================
         Apache Kafka clients CVE-2025-27817 and CVE-2025-27818 —
         arbitrary file read / SSRF (27817) and SASL JAAS RCE via
         LdapLoginModule (27818). Both affect kafka-clients 3.7.2
         and earlier.

         References:
           https://kafka.apache.org/cve-list  (Apache Kafka project)
           CVE-2025-27817 — arbitrary file read / SSRF via certain
             Kafka admin client configuration paths
           CVE-2025-27818 — SASL JAAS RCE via the
             LdapLoginModule configuration

         Status:           Acceptance — runtime conditions absent
         Approved by:      Security Review Board (Code Review CP9 FINAL)
         Approval date:    2026-05-25
         Next review:      2026-08-25 (90-day cadence)

         Why suppress rather than upgrade
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         The upstream OSS fix is kafka-clients 3.9.1 or 4.0.0.
         CardDemo consumes kafka-clients transitively via Spring
         Kafka, which (at the Spring Boot 3.3.13 BOM-pinned
         version) bundles kafka-clients 3.7.2. Independently
         overriding the kafka-clients version while keeping the
         BOM-pinned Spring Kafka is officially DISCOURAGED by the
         Spring Kafka project because the two artifacts are
         released as a compatibility-matched pair. The planned
         remediation is to upgrade Spring Kafka to the first
         release that ships kafka-clients 3.9.1+ (a coordinated
         BOM upgrade that will follow Spring Boot 3.5.x
         migration).

         Active runtime mitigations (defence in depth)
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
           1. PRIMARY (CVE-2025-27817) — Runtime exploit
              conditions ABSENT. CVE-2025-27817 requires invoking
              the Kafka AdminClient with attacker-controlled
              configuration (specifically the CONFIG-related admin
              APIs that load external resources). CardDemo does
              NOT use the AdminClient. Verified by:
                $ grep -rn "AdminClient\|KafkaAdmin\|
                  CreateTopicsResult\|DescribeTopicsResult" \
                  src/main/java/com/awsm2/carddemo/
                  -> 0 matches
              CardDemo uses only:
                - ProducerFactory + KafkaTemplate (publisher)
                - ConsumerFactory + ConcurrentKafkaListener
                  ContainerFactory (consumer)
                - @KafkaListener on a fixed set of @Configuration-
                  declared topics
              Topic creation is delegated to MSK cluster
              provisioning (infrastructure/terraform/msk.tf).

           2. PRIMARY (CVE-2025-27818) — Runtime exploit
              conditions ABSENT. CVE-2025-27818 requires BOTH:
                (a) The vulnerable login module
                    `com.sun.security.auth.module.LdapLoginModule`
                    (or another login module with comparable
                    URL-loading semantics) to be configured via
                    `sasl.jaas.config`, AND
                (b) Attacker-controlled input to `sasl.jaas.config`
                    or to a referenced URL.
              CardDemo authenticates to MSK using AWS_MSK_IAM SASL
              with the `IAMLoginModule` from the
              `software.amazon.msk:aws-msk-iam-auth` artifact
              (declared in pom.xml). The `IAMLoginModule` is an
              AWS-managed JAAS module that resolves credentials
              via the AWS SDK v2 DefaultCredentialsProvider chain
              (ECS task role in prod) and signs broker
              authentication with AWS Signature Version 4 — it
              has NO equivalent of the LdapLoginModule's
              URL-loading code path that the CVE exploits.
              Verified by:
                $ grep -rnE "LdapLoginModule|com\.sun\.security\.
                  auth\.module" src/ -> 0 matches
                  (confirming the vulnerable login module is
                   NOT on the classpath as configured)
                $ grep -rn "sasl.jaas.config" \
                  src/main/resources/application-*.yml
                  -> only statically-declared
                  "software.amazon.msk.auth.iam.IAMLoginModule
                   required;" entries, never accepting attacker
                  input
                $ grep -rnE "loginModule|LoginModule" \
                  src/main/java/com/awsm2/carddemo/
                  -> only Javadoc comments in
                  KafkaConfig.java; no Java code dynamically
                  builds or loads a LoginModule
              Both gating conditions of the CVE are therefore
              absent: the vulnerable login module is not invoked,
              and the JAAS configuration is static (declared in
              version-controlled application-*.yml files, never
              constructed from runtime input).

           3. NETWORK ISOLATION — MSK brokers
              (infrastructure/terraform/msk.tf) are deployed in
              private subnets with security groups that admit
              traffic only from the ECS task security group;
              there is no public network path through which an
              attacker-controlled SASL JAAS configuration could
              reach the broker even if the application code
              were modified to accept one.

           4. DETECTION — The owasp Maven profile would surface
              any future inclusion of an AdminClient-using
              dependency or any code that introduces JAAS
              LoginModule overrides (the suppression is scoped
              narrowly to kafka-clients 3.7.2 and would NOT
              cover a regression to a different
              vulnerable-pattern artifact).

         Suppression scope
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         This suppression matches kafka-clients (and the
         transitively-pulled kafka-server / kafka-storage-api
         artifacts present only in the test classpath) for
         versions in the affected range — currently 3.7.2 is
         the resolved version. The suppression covers both
         CVE-2025-27817 and CVE-2025-27818 in a single entry
         because both share the same active-mitigation
         analysis (no AdminClient usage, no JAAS overrides) and
         the same upgrade-path planning (deferred to Spring
         Kafka 3.9.1+ compatibility).

         Re-evaluation triggers
         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
           * 2026-08-25 mandatory review (90-day cadence)
           * Earlier review if Spring Kafka publishes a release
             with kafka-clients 3.9.1+ (the planned upgrade)
           * Earlier review if the application code is modified
             to introduce AdminClient usage or JAAS LoginModule
             configuration
           * Earlier review if MSK authentication is changed
             from AWS_MSK_IAM to a SASL/SCRAM or SASL/PLAIN
             mechanism that uses JAAS LoginModules
         ============================================================ -->
    <suppress>
        <notes><![CDATA[
            CVE-2025-27817 (file read / SSRF via Kafka AdminClient
